.chapter 1
.h1 Purpose and scope [beginning]
Strata is a document processor for long structured documents, written in C
from 8 to 19 November 2026.  The project maintainer, a human, set the goals
and made the decisions.  Assistant sessions, instances of the AI model Claude,
did the jobs.  This document records method, measurements and open items.  Each
number names its source.
.h2 Structure of this document [beginning-reading]
This document is also a sample of a structured document.  {Tab:features} lists
the features that it uses.  Open it in the Editor and collapse it with the
outline commands; print it to see the generated parts.
.tabcap features Structured-document features used in this document
.table 20 44
Feature | Where
Sections of four levels | {x:beginning-where}
Contents, lists, index | Generated by the Printer, not stored as text
Cross references | Section numbers, for example {x:latex}
Footnotes | {x:beginning-goal}, {x:probing}
Notes | {x:probing-limits}
Line numbers | Left margin, restarting on every page
Header and footer | Every page after the title page
Conditional text | {x:conditional-this}
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE = release
This copy is the release edition: it has eight chapters.
.else
This copy is the developer edition: it has nine chapters.  The release edition
omits chapter 8 and four sections of other chapters.
.endif
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
{X:machines} describes how the work was divided between the project maintainer
and the assistant sessions.
.endif
.h3 Structured documents [beginning-structured]
A structured document is a tree of sections.  A section has a headline, blocks
of text and subsections.  The Editor numbers sections ({c:1}, {c:1.1},
{c:1.1.1}) and renumbers them when a section moves.{fn:A block is a paragraph,
a list item or a table row.  A block can be split into columns, and a table is
a row of blocks that have columns.}
.h4 Position [beginning-where]
This passage is {x:beginning-where}, a section of the fourth level inside
{x:beginning-structured}.  The header of each page shows the title of the
section in which the page begins, and the footer shows the page number.
.h2 Goal and clean room practice [beginning-goal]
.ix Clean room
The goal is a program that reads and writes the documents of a DOS word
processor of the late 1980s and works the same way: the same keys, menus,
panels, structured documents, tables, markers and outline commands.  The
inputs were the install disks, manuals and sample documents of the old program
and its behaviour in an emulator.  No code of the old program was
copied.{fn:Clean room design is described, with its legal background, in
Samuelson and Scotchmer, The Law and Economics of Reverse Engineering, Yale
Law Journal 111 (2002) 1575; the decision Sega Enterprises Ltd. v. Accolade,
Inc., 977 F.2d 1510 (9th Cir. 1992), on disassembly for the purpose of
compatibility; and Rod Canion, Open: How Compaq Ended IBM's PC Domination and
Helped Invent Modern Computing (BenBella Books), which tells how the BIOS of a
compatible computer was written by a team that had not seen the original.
These are sources about the practice, not legal advice.}  The working rule, a
summary and not a quotation:
.quote
  The new program does not have to print like the old one, but it must read
  its files, keep what it does not understand, and write the files back
  unchanged.
.end
.h2 Timeline [beginning-timeline]
{Tab:timeline} gives the main steps by date, from the log of the repository.
The busiest days were 16 November (@commits_16@ commits), 17 November
(@commits_17@) and 18 November (@commits_18@).
.tabcap timeline Main steps by date
.table 13 48
Date | What was done
8 Nov | The install disks are put in the repository.
9 to 10 Nov | Loader and writer; markers; menus; macros; library.
11 to 12 Nov | Large document opens fast; its memory cut from
 | @early_mem_before@ to @early_mem_after@ MB; Viewer; Preview.
13 to 14 Nov | First manual; print to PDF; spell check; line numbering.
15 Nov | Conditional text; LaTeX marker; help file of 122 topics.
16 Nov | Undo and Redo; Compare Document Versions; colours; backups.
17 Nov | Headers and footers; index and lists; borders; templates.
18 Nov | Memory and speed measured and reduced; envelopes; dictionaries.
19 Nov | Borders joined in print; cursor place saved; samples.
.end
